        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="JeqwwW2tFP1rF5G0HEiH-7" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;textShadow=0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;labelBackgroundColor=none;" value="&lt;h3 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;The Berkeley Software&lt;br&gt;Distribution&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;b&gt;Berkeley Software Distribution &lt;/b&gt;(&lt;b&gt;BSD&lt;/b&gt;), aka Berkeley Unix, is a Unix OS developed and distributed by the Computer Systems Research Group at the University of California, Berkeley. It is an improved derivative of AT&amp;amp;T&#39;s original Unix, based on the source code.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The development is led by Bill Joy, who adds&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;virtual memory&lt;/i&gt;&amp;nbsp;capability to Unix running on a &lt;b&gt;VAX-11&lt;/b&gt; computer. BSD gains widespread adoption by workstation vendors in the form of proprietary Unix distributions. BSD becomes the most widely used Unix variant in academic institutions, used for the study of operating systems.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;FreeBSD&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;is one of the most famous open-source descendants of BSD. In current day, BSD-derived code has been incorporated into many operating systems, such as macOS and iOS.&lt;/p&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="381.75" width="222.56" x="200" y="190" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
